ZZACWT is an CW training application developed by Philip Rose for personal use.

I am now making this publicly available.

Windows Installation
--------------------

A file ZZACWT-[version].msi is available from https://sourceforge/projects/zzacwt. 
Please follow instructions to install this. ZZACWT will be installed in 
"C:\Program Files\GM3ZZA\ZZACWT\". 

The source may be cloned from https://github.com/pvrose/zzacwt. 
However this has dependencies on the following libraries: 
FLTK. 
The documentation also requires the use of Doxygen and LateX.

It may be built using CMake under MSVC. See the appropriate section in the User Guide.

Building and debugging with Visual Studio Code
-----------------------------------------------

Install Visual Studio Code with the Microsoft CMake Tools and C/C++ extensions,
then open the repository folder. CMake Tools will read the included CMake
presets. Choose a configure preset from the status bar (for example,
"x64-debug" or "x64-release" on Windows, or "linux-debug" on Linux), then use
the CMake: Build command or press F7. Select the matching "ZZACWT" launch
configuration and press F5 to debug.

On Windows, start VS Code from an x64 Visual Studio Developer PowerShell or
Developer Command Prompt so the compiler and Windows SDK environment is set.
The Windows presets expect the same local dependencies and paths as the
existing CMake setup: Visual Studio C++ tools, vcpkg, PortAudio, FFTW, and FLTK.
Linux builds require the dependencies listed below. The CMake preset format
requires CMake 3.21 or newer.



Linux Installation
------------------

ZZACWT is available in source form. It may be cloned from 
"https://github.com/pvrose/zzacwt". 
It has dependencies on the external libraries: fltk and 
nlohmann/json. To date this has only been developed on Debian trixie.

FLTK can either be compiled from source cloned from github (https://github.com/fltk/fltk) or 
installed form a tarball. It is also available in most distros. Version 1.4.3 
or greater is required.

Nlohmann/json is available in most distros.

Doxygen is available in most distros.

The build and install processes use CMake.

For more details see the installation section in the User Guide.